LEAD = {
    "headline": "A MILLION SUBSCRIBERS, MORE THAN 60 MILLION VIEWS, TEN YEARS, AND THE FIRST REASON GIVEN FOR CLOSING THE CHANNEL WAS THAT SQUARESPACE IS PULLING OUT",
    "deck": "Mango Street has taught photography on YouTube for ten years and across nearly 400 videos.  Its two founders have said it is ending, The Publish Press reported it yesterday, and the first reason they gave was that Squarespace is pulling out before the end of the year.  A million subscribers were still there.  What left was one line in somebody else's marketing budget.",
    "stamps": [
        ("THE PUBLISH PRESS · 21 SEP", "https://news.thepublishpress.com/p/live-from-new-york-it-s"),
        ("MANGO STREET · YOUTUBE", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SWxtsTfZOao"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Mango Street is Rachel Gulotta and Daniel Inskeep, who have been making photography tutorials for a decade.  <mark>Ten years, nearly 400 videos, a million subscribers, more than 60 million views.</mark>  They announced the end in a video titled the end of mango street, and The Publish Press reported it yesterday.",
        "The reasons came in an order, and the order is the item.  <mark>First, Squarespace is pulling out before year-end.  Second, views and engagement had been declining for a while.  Third, the channel was built almost entirely on long-form with no short-form hedge.</mark>  Inskeep on the decline: for the past couple years we felt like there was a dark cloud over our channel, videos weren't getting a lot of comments or views.  And his advice to other creators: diversify your forms of revenue, you never know when it's going to disappear.",
        "Before the mechanism, the honest part.  <mark>We have one outlet and the creators' own video.  No revenue figures were published — nothing on what share of the channel's income Squarespace was, no fee, no term, no notice period.</mark>  Squarespace has not commented.  We went looking for reporting that Squarespace is cutting creator spend generally and found none from any named publication, so do not read this as a brand pulling out of the category.  Read it as one renewal not happening.",
        "What survives is the structure, and it is the thing worth your morning.  An advertising read on a 20-minute photography tutorial is not a bonus on top of the platform money.  <mark>On a teaching channel in a small niche the sponsor is structurally the load-bearing revenue line rather than a bonus on top of the platform money, because the platform pays on views while the advertiser pays on the audience being the exact people it wants.</mark>  We have no figure for this channel, which is precisely why the closure is the only evidence anybody gets.  The anchor advertiser leaving is not a line item going down.  It is the line item going away.",
        "Now the half that changes what you do, because it is the buyer's half.  <mark>If your exit closes a channel, you were not buying a media placement at market price.  You were funding the asset.</mark>  That is a different thing and it carries different leverage — on price, on term, on what you get for renewing — and few buyers price it that way.  Worth noting we wrote on the seventeenth that Squarespace also funds a coffee channel of unusual technical depth.  We have no visibility into that budget, and that is the point: decisions inside one company's marketing plan are deciding which channels exist.",
        "It cuts the other way too.  If you are the anchor sponsor on a channel you depend on, the audience you are buying is sitting on a single point of failure, and you are the point of failure.  <mark>We have never seen a brand audit its creator roster for this.</mark>  You can find out in an afternoon by asking one question, and the answer tells you which of your partnerships is actually a dependency.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("1", "advertiser whose exit was the first reason the founders gave for closing the channel"),
        ("1M", "subscribers and more than 60 million lifetime views attached to an archive that stops getting new uploads"),
        ("0", "revenue figures published, so nobody outside can say what share of the business that sponsor was"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Every figure here comes from The Publish Press's reporting of the creators' own announcement video.  No financial detail was published at all — no fee, no term, no notice period, no revenue split, no share of income — so no claim about how much of the business the sponsorship represented can be tested, and we have not made one.  Squarespace has not commented and we found no named publication reporting that it is reducing creator sponsorship generally; the withdrawal is stated by the creators about their own deal only.  The subscriber, video and view counts are as reported and are YouTube's rounded figures rather than exact counts.  The three reasons are given in the order the creators gave them, which is not evidence of their relative weight.",
    "so_what": "On a small-niche teaching channel the sponsor is usually the business, not a bonus on top of it, because the platform pays for views while the advertiser pays for the audience being precisely the right people.  That makes an anchor advertiser structurally load-bearing in a way the rate card never says out loud.  Both sides have been pricing these deals as media placements when the money is closer to funding than to buying.  The brand that works that out gets better terms, and the brand that does not eventually loses a channel it had come to rely on.",
    "do_this": "Pull your creator roster this week and ask every partner one question: what share of your channel's revenue does our deal represent.  Anyone above roughly a third is a dependency rather than a placement, and you should be negotiating term and price on that basis and staggering their renewal away from your other partners'.  Then flag the ones where your exit would end the channel, because that is your leverage and also your risk.",
}

